; Stacked tag encodings — a tag applied to an already-tagged value, reached through alias/rule
; reference stacking (literal `#6.24(#6.258(..))` is parse-rejected as doubly-nested, so the stack
; is expressed by a tag rule wrapping another tag rule / a tag-set idiom).
;
; Under --preserve-encodings each tag LEVEL needs its OWN encoding member or the two levels collide
; on one name and the encoding struct fails to compile. Levels count OUTSIDE-IN: level 1 keeps the
; historical `{name}_tag_encoding`, level k >= 2 gets `{name}_tag{k}_encoding`.
;
; - `homogeneous_inner` / `homogeneous` — two MANDATORY tags stack (24 over 258), both lowering to
;   `Option<cbor_event::Sz>`; pins `inner_tag_encoding` + `inner_tag2_encoding` and that the nested
;   deserialize match arms bind distinct sizes (`tag_enc` / `tag_enc2`) instead of shadowing.
; - `heterogeneous_set` / `heterogeneous` — a MANDATORY outer tag 24 over the optional-tag set idiom
;   (258-or-untagged): level 1 is `Option<cbor_event::Sz>` (mandatory) and level 2 is
;   `TagPresenceEncoding` (optional), pinning that the two encoding kinds sit at their own levels.
; - `elem_tagged` / `outer_over_array` — a MANDATORY outer tag 24 over an ARRAY whose ELEMENT carries
;   its own tag 258. The array element opens a fresh `{field}_elem` name namespace, so the element's
;   tag is LEVEL 1 THERE (`{field}_elem_tag_encoding`) even though the field crossed tag 24 outside the
;   array — the tag depth must RESET at the element boundary on the serialize side exactly as it does
;   in the encoding-member derivation, or the write reads a depth-inflated var the struct never minted.
; - `holder` — embeds all three in record-field position so member-side (de)serialize compiles + roundtrips.
homogeneous_inner = #6.258([* uint])
homogeneous = #6.24(homogeneous_inner)
heterogeneous_set = #6.258([* uint]) / [* uint]
heterogeneous = #6.24(heterogeneous_set)
elem_tagged = #6.258([* uint])
outer_over_array = #6.24([* elem_tagged])
holder = [h: homogeneous, s: heterogeneous, a: outer_over_array]
